WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guests
are the writers, producers,

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:05.000
and stars of the new movie
"Dicks: The Musical,"

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.434
which is in theaters
everywhere now.

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:07.667
Let's take a look.

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:10.701
-This is crazy, right?
-Totally crazy.

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:11.968
I mean, what are the odds

00:00:11.968 --> 00:00:13.300
of working with
your long-lost twin?

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.100
-I don't know. 1 in 4, 3 in 4?
It's crazy.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.467
-Crazy. But wait...

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:20.534
I can't believe Mom and Dad
split us up.

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:23.234
Like, is that abuse?
Did they abuse us?

00:00:23.234 --> 00:00:25.234
-We definitely
experienced "abusement."

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:27.667
-Oh, my God.
-I mean, don't get me wrong.

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:29.267
My life is awesome.
-No, no, no, no, no.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:30.934
M-M-Mine is awesome, too.

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:32.634
-I have an amazing job.
-Amazing job.

00:00:32.634 --> 00:00:34.133
-Always dating
some big [Bleep] lady.

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.868
-Stunning big [Bleep] lady.
-I always get the things I want.

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:39.734
Except for a real family.
-Single-parent homes

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.033
are not real families.
That's true.

00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:44.033
Hmm.
-Hmm.

00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.033
-What do we do?

00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:50.601
-Please welcome to the show
Aaron Jackson and Josh Sharp,

00:00:50.601 --> 00:00:51.901
everybody!

00:00:51.901 --> 00:00:54.267
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:57.467
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:06.367
♪♪

00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:08.334
Welcome to the show,
gentlemen.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:09.934
-Thank you.
-And congratulations

00:01:09.934 --> 00:01:11.367
on this incredible film.

00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:13.000
We've been lucky to have
some of your costars.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.634
Bowen Yang was here,
the great Nathan Lane was here.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.067
-Uh-huh.
-Yes.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.400
-And then there are
some other --

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.868
Mega Mullally's in it.
Megan Thee Stallion is in it.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:21.968
-Yeah, Thee Stallion
and Thee Mullally.

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:24.067
-Yeah, really, uh --
-You almost have,

00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:25.901
like, the royal flush
of the whole cast.

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:27.100
-You did an incredible job.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.067
It must be thrilling
to get people like that

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.601
to do this
very singular piece of work.

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:33.834
-Sure. That's a nice way
to say "crazy."

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:36.767
-[ Laughs ]
-So it is so deeply crazy.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.901
Nathan Lane, who's done
so many crazy things,

00:01:38.901 --> 00:01:42.067
basically said it's the craziest
thing he's ever done.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:44.334
-A badge of honor
to hear that from Daddy.

00:01:44.334 --> 00:01:46.501
-Yeah, truly,
we wear that with pride.

00:01:46.501 --> 00:01:50.067
-So, this is a show you guys
used to do onstage at UCB?

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.334
-Yes.
-True.

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:53.434
-And I don't want to give
too much of the plot away,

00:01:53.434 --> 00:01:56.234
but it is sort of
a "Parent Trap"-type film.

00:01:56.234 --> 00:01:58.501
-It's true. We're using
"The Parent Trap" as a device

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:02.067
because it's really one of the
oldest story structures we have.

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:03.200
There's, like,
"Romeo and Juliet,"

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.000
"The Odyssey,"
and "The Parent Trap."

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.968
They're sort of accepted
narrative modalities.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:09.834
-And you could see
from that clip,

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:13.434
we're sort of -- we're playing
heterosexual men.

00:02:13.434 --> 00:02:16.167
But we are not heterosexual men.
-We're not.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:19.334
-So it's actually
very brave and...

00:02:19.334 --> 00:02:21.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:23.167
Yeah! Thank you.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.834
-In fact, the opening
of the film,

00:02:25.834 --> 00:02:27.834
just in case -- you're
not breaking news here,

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:29.367
because I believe
the opening of the film

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:31.133
has a title card
saying how brave it is.

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:33.767
-We want Oscars bad.
-Yes.

00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:35.200
-And this is
how you win an Oscar.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.968
-I believe you say
it's the first film

00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:40.267
ever written by a gay writer.
-Yes.

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:41.767
-I know your show
has researchers.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:45.200
We don't, so if that is wrong,
don't tell on us.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.934
-Not on us.
-Not on us.

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:48.267
How do we do, playing straight?

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.501
-Oh, my God,
it's so believable.

00:02:50.501 --> 00:02:54.434
-Does it feel lived in?
-It felt very great. Thank you.

00:02:54.434 --> 00:02:56.534
It was so nice to see,
finally,

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:58.133
it's, like, see
myself represented.

00:02:58.133 --> 00:03:00.167
-Yeah, totally.
-You know what I mean?

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:03.801
-I know. I lived as straight
for like 18 years.

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:05.634
So it was Method,
the work I had done.

00:03:05.634 --> 00:03:08.801
-It showed up.
It really showed up on-screen.

00:03:08.801 --> 00:03:11.000
-You call them
"big [Bleep] ladies," right?

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.100
-We do.
-That's an industry term?

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.367
-We do -- industry, the fellas.

00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:18.067
I mean, you -- of the many
things you had to do,

00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:21.934
you actually wrote a rap and
then had to send the lyrics...

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:24.000
-Yes.
-...to Megan Thee Stallion.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.767
-She's -- She's rapped before.
-Yeah.

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:29.133
And she's rather good at it.
-Yeah.

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:31.400
-A lot of people say it's one
of the things she's known for.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.067
-Yeah.
-I would say, top 4 or 5 things.

00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:37.267
But then our gay asses
wrote a bunch of words,

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:38.801
and we're like,
"She won't say them."

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:41.033
And then she rapped
every single one of those words.

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.868
-There's, like --
There's amazing footage of Josh

00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:45.767
just, like, rapping it
into a cellphone

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.133
to, like, send to her manager.

00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:49.367
And then she just listened
to that rap

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:51.033
and was like,
"Great, I'll do it."

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:53.334
And when she showed up on-set,
she was like --

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:55.133
we were rehearsing --
she was like,

00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:56.734
"I just want to say,
people say I'm dirty,

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:59.467
but y'all are dirty."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:02.534
-Profound badge of honor
to have Miss The Stallion

00:04:02.534 --> 00:04:06.601
say, "You have done me right.
This is dirty."

00:04:06.601 --> 00:04:08.200
-Do you have trepidation
when you send --

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.801
when you record a rap?
-Well, I was told

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.801
it would just go
to her manager and not her.

00:04:11.801 --> 00:04:13.534
So I was hoodwinked.
And then she showed up

00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:15.167
and was like,
"You got bars, young man."

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:17.567
-Yeah.
-So, what did you think?

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:20.634
Did you think the manager
would then rap it to her?

00:04:20.634 --> 00:04:22.601
-I don't know what I thought.
I thought it was just like --

00:04:22.601 --> 00:04:25.534
Really, we kept expecting her
to be -- to say no.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:27.300
Every point, I was like,
"Someone will hear this

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.167
and go, "She's out.
She can't possibly do this."

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:30.767
-It's really wonderful,
because, you know --

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:32.868
and it's very early --
when she shows up,

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:36.701
she shows up with an energy
where she's like, "I am game.

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.234
I am in on this.
-She's so cool.

00:04:38.234 --> 00:04:40.634
I mean, she was amazing.
-And now, Nathan Lane

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:43.667
talked about his scene partners
are the Sewer Boys.

00:04:43.667 --> 00:04:46.100
I don't want to give too much
away about the Sewer Boys,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.501
but he did bring them
on the show.

00:04:47.501 --> 00:04:48.934
-Yeah.
-The Sewer Boys are

00:04:48.934 --> 00:04:53.868
sort of a subterranean creatures
that he adopted

00:04:53.868 --> 00:04:56.567
and keeps in his apartment.
-So beautiful and also pretty.

00:04:56.567 --> 00:04:58.400
Gorgeous.
-Can I correct the record?

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.167
Everyone, including
on this program,

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:02.868
has called them puppets.
These are actors that we cast.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:06.000
-Yeah, they came in,
they auditioned for the role.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:07.467
They just brought something.
-They did a great --

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:09.267
So, Nathan brought them
to the show,

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:11.300
and then they actually
stuck around our offices.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.300
-Yeah, they work hard.
-Hire them for admin.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.567
-Hire them for admin
or for acting.

00:05:16.567 --> 00:05:17.868
-They're so good at admin,
though.

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:19.767
-They are, they are.

00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:23.567
-We read Chalamet for them.
-Yeah, Jennifer Lopez.

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:25.534
-Chalamet came in
for a Sewer Boy?

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:26.834
-Yeah.
-And he didn't get it?

00:05:26.834 --> 00:05:29.067
-This close.
-They have the authenticity.

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:31.601
-He is very good.
-He read Fallon.

00:05:31.601 --> 00:05:33.267
-Oh, really?
-He's mad.

00:05:33.267 --> 00:05:34.901
That's why we're here.
-Oh, that's why you're here.

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:36.334
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:36.334 --> 00:05:39.434
-There's some really -- So,
because, again, "Parent Trap,"

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:41.968
you then switch wigs,
or you switch hair.

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:43.234
So, it's wigs.
-Yeah.

00:05:43.234 --> 00:05:45.400
-And they're even, I will say --

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.133
like, even Nathan's character
points out that the wigs --

00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:49.767
it looks like a cheap wig.
-Yeah.

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:51.501
-It does not feel like you
maybe spent a ton of money

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:54.434
on the wigs in this film.
Here you guys are with opposite.

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:56.167
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.167 --> 00:05:58.834
So, I know this is both --
Look, this was a --

00:05:58.834 --> 00:06:00.968
-Wait, could you even tell
that that was us playing?

00:06:00.968 --> 00:06:03.267
-Who's to say?
-Could you even...

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:04.868
-Who is who?

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:07.534
-Now, when did you --
It is very funny

00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:08.968
because, obviously,
the first time you guys

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:10.501
see each other in the movie,
you're taken aback

00:06:10.501 --> 00:06:12.367
by how you look identical
to one another.

00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:16.434
Do you remember the day when,
as young improvisers,

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:17.968
you first came up
with the idea of like,

00:06:17.968 --> 00:06:21.000
"Wouldn't it be funny if we had
this as the jumping-off point?"

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.601
-Yeah, we were like, "For sure,
this will one day

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:25.133
become a movie."
-Yes, this is the normal,

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:26.667
like, how most movies are made.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:29.300
You do them in the basement
of a grocery store,

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:31.467
and then that's
"Kramer vs. Kramer."

00:06:31.467 --> 00:06:33.734
-Yeah, that was
started that way.

00:06:33.734 --> 00:06:35.100
-Have your parents
seen the film?

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:36.601
-They have.
-And?

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:38.868
-They're, like,
very lovely Southern people

00:06:38.868 --> 00:06:40.200
who you'd think
wouldn't like this,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.067
and they love it.
And I think, furthermore,

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:43.834
they love that we have
health insurance.

00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:45.667
-That's great.
-I think that is, like...

00:06:45.667 --> 00:06:48.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
-You guys deserve it.

00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:50.267
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:52.701
-And I also want to note
that the Sewer Boys,

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:55.534
which we had here
and then we returned to A24,

00:06:55.534 --> 00:06:59.367
which made the film.
You wanted to keep --

00:06:59.367 --> 00:07:01.267
-Oh, yes.
So, the last day of filming,

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:03.000
everyone keeps
calling them grotesque.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.667
We find them to be beautiful.
-I don't see that.

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.200
-I don't see --
I don't see that.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.234
And the last day of filming,
we had this whole plan.

00:07:08.234 --> 00:07:09.601
We're like,
"We're gonna steal the puppets

00:07:09.601 --> 00:07:11.434
so we can keep them."
A24 caught us.

00:07:11.434 --> 00:07:12.868
They're like,
"You cannot do that.

00:07:12.868 --> 00:07:14.133
We need them for reshoots.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.501
They might one day
go on 'Seth.'"

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.567
And then they said,
"We promise you,

00:07:17.567 --> 00:07:19.767
when this movie cycle is done,
you will get them.

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:22.367
No one [Bleep] wants them
but you."

00:07:22.367 --> 00:07:24.167
[ Laughter ]

00:07:24.167 --> 00:07:26.868
-Well, it's nice to know
they're finally gonna end up

00:07:26.868 --> 00:07:29.367
in a loving home.
-Gonna be administration.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:31.868
-Yes.
They're are assistants now.

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:33.234
-Guys, congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:07:33.234 --> 00:07:35.234
-It's so much fun
to watch a film

00:07:35.234 --> 00:07:37.567
that's unlike
anything I've ever seen.

00:07:37.567 --> 00:07:39.601
And it's great to have you both.
-Thank you.

00:07:39.601 --> 00:07:41.300
-And they're doing --
We were asked to say

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.467
they're doing sing-alongs.
-Sing-along screening.

00:07:43.467 --> 00:07:45.634
-You guys are gonna do
sing-along screenings

00:07:45.634 --> 00:07:47.167
of the movie.
That's gonna start this week?

00:07:47.167 --> 00:07:49.634
-This week, they're doing
with the words on the screen.

00:07:49.634 --> 00:07:51.701
This is up
for "Oppenheimer," too.

00:07:51.701 --> 00:07:52.801
-Yes, us and "Oppenheimer."
-They're gonna do

00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:54.567
a sing-along "Oppenheimer"?
-Yeah.

00:07:54.567 --> 00:07:56.400
-Great. They should.
Thank you guys so much

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.400
for being here.
Aaron Jackson, Josh Sharp.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:59.834
"Dicks: The Musical"
is in theaters,

00:07:59.834 --> 00:08:01.300
and the soundtrack
is available now.

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:02.801
Stick around. We'll be right
back with more "Late Night."

